## Adapter mutant A6 — name-derived (`displayName.startsWith("Alternate")`)

Surfaced at the boundary gate. The synthetic fixture's alternate is literally
NAMED "Alternate", so both shipped pins score identically on it. Closed against
the REAL bundled catalog, where the docs-loader strips the game's "Alternate: "
prefix — `Alternate: Iron Alloy Ingot` is stored as `Iron Alloy Ingot`.

  MUTATION  src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.ts:980
    -  isAlternate: candidate.isAlternate,
    +  isAlternate: candidate.displayName.startsWith("Alternate"),

  COMPILE CHECK (a crashing mutant proves nothing)
    $ npx tsc -b   ->  exit 0, CLEAN. The kill below is behavioural.

  CONTROL — do the two SHIPPED pins see it?  NO, BOTH STILL PASS:
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts \
        -t "carries isAlternate from the RECIPE"
      Tests  1 passed | 93 skipped (94)
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/AltCompare.test.tsx -t "marks the ALTERNATE row"
      Tests  1 passed | 11 skipped (12)
    ^ confirms the reviewer's finding exactly, and confirms the new row is NOT
      redundant with the existing two.

  THE NEW PIN — KILLED:
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts \
        -t "flags isAlternate against REAL parsed names"
      x flags isAlternate against REAL parsed names, not a name prefix (#116)
      AssertionError: expected [ false, false, false, false, false ]
                      to deeply equal [ false, true, true, true, true ]
      Tests  1 failed | 93 skipped (94)

    The received vector is all-false, which is precisely what this mutant does
    in PRODUCTION: it would mark nothing, ever, while every synthetic test
    stayed green. That is the failure mode worth the extra row -- a total,
    silent feature no-op rather than a subtle mis-mark.

  RESTORE
    $ cp /tmp/cba.bak src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.ts
    $ npm test        ->  Test Files 33 passed | Tests 910 passed (910)
    $ npm run check   ->  clean (tsc + eslint + prettier)

  The second assertion in that row pins the PREMISE (no parsed name starts with
  "Alternate"), so the row cannot rot into a tautology if the parser ever stops
  stripping the prefix -- it would then fail loudly instead of silently ceasing
  to discriminate.

## Adapter mutant M7 — id-derived (`candidate.id.includes("alternate")`)

Surfaced by the DIFF-stage simplify pass while testing whether the real-catalog
pin could subsume the synthetic one (it proposed the fold, then broke it itself).

**More plausible than A6, not less.** The parser derives the real flag as
`id.includes("alternate_") || displayName.startsWith("Alternate:")`
(docs-loader), so an implementer re-deriving it inline rather than reading
`candidate.isAlternate` writes exactly the id form — it mirrors the parser.

  MUTATION  src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.ts:980
    -  isAlternate: candidate.isAlternate,
    +  isAlternate: candidate.id.includes("alternate"),

  COMPILE CHECK
    $ npx tsc -b   ->  exit 0, CLEAN. Behavioural kill.

  MEASURED — the two fixtures are blind in OPPOSITE directions:
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts \
        -t "flags isAlternate against REAL parsed names"
      Tests  1 passed | 93 skipped (94)          <- pin 3 SURVIVES
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts \
        -t "carries isAlternate from the RECIPE"
      AssertionError: expected [ false, false ] to deeply equal [ false, true ]
      Tests  1 failed | 93 skipped (94)          <- pin 1 KILLS
    $ npx vitest run src/ui/AltCompare.test.tsx -t "marks the ALTERNATE row"
      Tests  1 failed | 11 skipped (12)          <- pin 2 KILLS

  WHY neither FIXTURE, AS THEY STAND, is removable:

    fixture    ids                 names        blind to      catches
    ---------  ------------------  -----------  ------------  ------------
    synthetic  r_std / r_alt       "Alternate"  A6 (name)     M7 (id)
    real       alternate_*         stripped     M7 (id)       A6 (name)

    The synthetic fixture has a name matching the alternate convention but an id
    that does not; the real one has the inverse. Each is the other's guard, so
    BOTH FIXTURES stay.

  A CLAIM THIS ENTRY ORIGINALLY MADE, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG:

    It said "no two-pin subset covers the ground." That is FALSE, and this
    file's own entries are the counterexample: pin 1 kills nothing pin 2 does
    not also kill (pin 2 runs the same synthetic fixture THROUGH the adapter),
    so {pin 2, pin 3} kills all ten recorded mutants. Corrected rather than
    quietly dropped, because a future simplify pass that checks the claim would
    find it refuted here and might delete pin 1 for the reason this entry failed
    to give.

    Pin 1 stays for a reason that is NOT mutation coverage: it is a unit-level
    contract pin on `candidateRowsFor`'s output, so it survives the render
    ceasing to show a marker at all (mutant R3 is exactly that), and it localises
    a failure to the adapter instead of to a rendered string.

  A CHEAPER SHAPE, CONSIDERED AND NOT TAKEN:

    Rename the synthetic fixture's alternate displayName ("Alternate" ->
    anything else) and pin 1 alone kills BOTH A6 and M7 — a two-pin shape.
    The r1 waiver priced this as touching "a fixture recipe shared by many
    pins"; that was OVERSTATED — grep confirms exactly one `recipeName`
    assertion in the file, and it is pin 3's real-catalog premise, not this
    fixture's name.

    The honest cost is TRANSCRIPT HYGIENE, not invalidation. An earlier draft of
    this entry said re-baselining "invalidates every mutant measurement, all ten
    of which would need re-running" — that was inflated, and inflated in the
    self-serving direction, since the paragraph's job is to justify NOT doing the
    work. In fact: R1/R2/R3 are measured by pin 2 against AltCompare's OWN `CAT`
    fixture, a different object, so they cannot change; A1-A5 and M7 assert pin
    1's name-independent vector `[false, true]`, so their outcomes hold. Exactly
    ONE measurement flips behaviourally — A6's control, where pin 1 goes from
    surviving to killing. Every transcript would be re-taken to stay current,
    which is bookkeeping, not ten re-measurements.

    Not taken anyway, because pin 3 is wanted on merits that survive the rename:

      (a) `chain-builder-adapter.test.ts:667` is the SOLE
          `recipeName.startsWith("Alternate")` assertion in the repo — the
          no-prefix premise against the REAL bundled catalog is pinned nowhere
          else. (`docs-loader.test.ts:167-178` pins the parser's strip + flag,
          but against the synthetic `DOCS_FRAGMENT`.)

      (b) Pin 3 is the only pin that kills A6, and the neighbouring
          `chain-builder-adapter.test.ts:628-629` CANNOT — it asserts the same
          [F,T,T,T,T] vector over the same five real candidates, but reads
          `candidateRecipesFor`, which returns catalog recipes and never touches
          the mutated line inside `candidateRowsFor`. Stated explicitly because a
          reader who finds :628-629 would otherwise assume it subsumes pin 3.

    An earlier draft claimed pin 3 was "the ONLY test anywhere that pins the real
    catalog's isAlternate vector and the no-prefix premise, so deleting it would
    leave the parser's contract unpinned." BOTH halves were false — :628-629
    pins that vector, and docs-loader.test.ts pins the parser's contract. Only
    the CONJUNCTION is unique. Recorded, not silently fixed: this is the second
    overreaching-absolute in this entry, and the failure mode is always the same
